---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import RegexTester from '@/components/tools/dev/RegexTester.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="regex-tester">
  <RegexTester client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to test a regular expression</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Type the pattern without slashes around it. The regex tester passes it directly to <code>new RegExp()</code>.</li>
      <li>Tick the flags you need. <code>g</code> is on by default, because without it only the first match is found.</li>
      <li>Paste the text to match against. Matches are highlighted as you type, in alternating colours so adjacent matches stay separate.</li>
      <li>Read the match table for exact positions and each captured group, numbered and named.</li>
      <li>Turn on the replace preview to see the result of <code>String.replace</code> with your replacement string.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Which regex flavour this is</h2>
    <p>
      The tester uses the JavaScript (ECMAScript) engine in your browser. The behaviour you see is the
      behaviour of your front-end code, your Node service and your JSON schema validator, including where
      JavaScript differs from PCRE. JavaScript has no atomic groups, no possessive quantifiers, no recursion
      and no <code>\A</code> or <code>\z</code> anchors. Its lookbehind can have variable length, which most
      other engines do not allow.
    </p>

    <h2>Catastrophic backtracking, and why this page cannot freeze</h2>
    <p>
      When a regular expression engine hits a dead end, it backtracks and tries another path. With nested
      quantifiers such as <code>(a+)+$</code>, the number of paths grows exponentially with the input length:
      25 characters of <code>a</code> followed by a <code>!</code> can take longer than the age of the
      universe. This is the mechanism behind ReDoS, a denial-of-service class that has taken down production
      services.
    </p>
    <p>
      A running regular expression cannot be interrupted on the main thread, so this tool runs each pattern
      in a Web Worker with a two-second budget. When the budget runs out, the tool stops the worker and
      explains why, and the tab does not lock up. If a pattern trips this guard, do not ship it anywhere user
      input can reach.
    </p>
    <h3>How to fix a backtracking pattern</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Make sure two parts of the pattern cannot match the same text. <code>(\s*\w+)*</code> is ambiguous; <code>[\s\w]*</code> is not.</li>
      <li>Replace <code>.*</code> between delimiters with a negated class: <code>"[^"]*"</code> in place of <code>".*"</code>.</li>
      <li>Anchor the pattern with <code>^</code> and <code>$</code> so the engine cannot retry from every position.</li>
      <li>For deeply structured input, write a parser in place of a regular expression.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Regex flags at a glance</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Flag</th><th>Name</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>g</code></td><td>global</td><td>Keep searching after the first match; required for replace-all and for the match list</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>i</code></td><td>ignoreCase</td><td>Case-insensitive matching</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>m</code></td><td>multiline</td><td><code>^</code> and <code>$</code> match at each line break, not only at the ends of the string</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>s</code></td><td>dotAll</td><td><code>.</code> also matches newlines</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>u</code></td><td>unicode</td><td>Treat the pattern as code points; required for <code>\p&#123;…&#125;</code> property escapes and correct handling of astral characters</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>y</code></td><td>sticky</td><td>Match only at <code>lastIndex</code>; useful for tokenisers, and finds far fewer matches than <code>g</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Named capture groups</h2>
    <p>
      Write <code>(?&lt;year&gt;\d&#123;4&#125;)</code> and the match table gets a column for
      <code>year</code>. In code you read it as <code>match.groups.year</code>, and in a replacement string as
      <code>$&lt;year&gt;</code>. Named groups make a pattern easier to edit: a new group in the middle
      renumbers all groups after it, and names stay the same.
    </p>

    <h2>A note on parsing HTML and email</h2>
    <p>
      The preset patterns are practical and not complete. The full grammar for an email address (RFC 5322)
      allows quoted strings, comments and nested parentheses. The "correct" regular expression for it is
      several thousand characters long and still does not prove the address exists. Match loosely, then
      verify by sending mail. HTML is not a regular language: a tag matcher is fine for a quick grep over a
      log file and wrong for anything that must be correct.
    </p>

    <h2>Your test data stays here</h2>
    <p>
      Your browser's own engine runs the patterns on the subject text inside a worker. Nothing is sent, so
      you can paste production log samples, customer records and unreleased content.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
